Patch 38. In the series since 2026-10-17, after patch 37.

AllocFixed (src/core/memory_posix.cpp) maps a fixed range with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, and commits
an already reserved range on its EEXIST (mprotect). Kernels before Linux 4.17 do not know the flag
and take the address as a hint: an occupied range is mapped somewhere else and that address is
returned. The heap then believes the page at its own address committed while it is still
PROT_NONE, and the first write there faults. Android devices still ship 4.9 and 4.14 kernels
(the Galaxy Tab S5e, Android 11: 4.9.227). Now, when the result is not the address asked for, it
is unmapped and the call goes on as EEXIST. Kernels from 4.17 on (every desktop Linux we build
for) never return another address with that flag, so nothing changes there.

Test: on the Tab S5e, a reservation then MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE at the same address returned another
address (Linux 7.0: EEXIST). unit_tests, which commit pages that earlier cases decommitted, ended
with SIGSEGV in "TranslateVirtual returns valid host pointer" when the random order put a
decommit first; with the patch two full runs pass there (the known output_stamp case aside). On
Linux, ctest as before.

diff --git a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
index ba533ec..86190c9 100644
--- a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
@@ -294,6 +294,16 @@ void* AllocFixed(void* base_address, size_t length, AllocationType allocation_ty
 #endif
 
   void* result = mmap(base_address, length, prot_initial, flags, -1, 0);
+#if defined(MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE) && REX_PLATFORM_LINUX
+  // Kernels before Linux 4.17 (Android devices still ship 4.9 and 4.14) do not know
+  // MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE and take the address as a hint: an occupied range is mapped
+  // somewhere else instead of failing with EEXIST.
+  if (result != MAP_FAILED && base_address && result != base_address) {
+    munmap(result, length);
+    result = MAP_FAILED;
+    errno = EEXIST;
+  }
+#endif
   if (result != MAP_FAILED) {
     return result;
   }
